From: GuildBound Dev <dev@guildbound.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Auction House design doc landed in status backlog (patch 0397)

--- a/guild-idler-status.md	2026-09-16 02:54:32.102522016 +0000
+++ b/guild-idler-status.md	2026-09-16 02:54:32.104032001 +0000
@@ -7703,19 +7703,143 @@
 - **Steam leaderboards** -- mentioned early as a distinct, larger feature;
   the Guild Rank tooltip in the Lore tab was deliberately worded to become
   literally true if this ever ships, without needing a rewrite.
+- **Auction House -- real cross-player trading, design locked, not yet
+  built.** WoW-style buyout marketplace, chosen explicitly over a
+  cheaper simulated/NPC-driven alternative after weighing the trade-off.
+  **Black Market is fully absorbed into it** rather than run as a
+  separate system -- a dev/system-seeded ("spoofed") listing is the same
+  data shape as a real player listing (same table, same buyout/escrow
+  path, just a special `sellerId`), so there's no case for two systems
+  doing the same job. The existing `blackMarketDiscount` upgrade maps
+  onto system-seeded listings specifically rather than needing a
+  redesign. Locked decisions:
+  - **Tradeable:** gear/equipment + consumables, every rarity including
+    Legendary. Gold-for-gold only -- no Renown or other currency is
+    tradeable.
+  - **Pricing: buyout only**, no bidding -- fits an idle game's async
+    play pattern (no need for two players online at once).
+  - **Undercut visibility on** -- players can see current listings/
+    lowest price for an item before pricing their own.
+  - **Duration: 24h base, up to 48h via a Guild upgrade**, same
+    cost-curve shape as Barracks/Treasury/etc. Whether the same upgrade
+    that gates access also unlocks 48h, or a separate follow-on tier
+    does, isn't decided.
+  - **Fees:** a deposit that scales with listing price (lost if the item
+    doesn't sell), plus a cut on successful sale. Exact percentages not
+    set.
+  - **Delivery via a new mailbox system.** The buyer's purchased item
+    lands directly in inventory at purchase time (they're online, doing
+    the buying, right now); only the *seller's* gold routes through the
+    mailbox, since they're not present for the transaction. Sold-but-
+    unclaimed gold and unsold, expired items both live there.
+  - **Listing cap per player:** "generous, 20+" -- exact number not
+    chosen.
+  - System-seeded stock pads supply during low real-listing periods;
+    whether that's a permanent mechanic or tapers off as real volume
+    grows is open. Whether to visibly tag it ("Broker Listing") or keep
+    it indistinguishable from real listings is a thematic call, not
+    made.
+  - **Anti-cheat: save-file signing/light protection only, deliberately
+    not** a server-authoritative trading ledger (gold/items only
+    becoming tradeable once earned through a server-witnessed event) --
+    considered and rejected as disproportionate engineering cost at
+    indie scale. Accepted risk applies both directions (a save-edited
+    account could list fabricated gear, or fabricate gold to buy real
+    players' real listings); revisit only if it's ever exploited at real
+    scale.
+
+  **Infrastructure (decided).** Self-hosted Node/TypeScript (Express or
+  Fastify) + Postgres on an existing server machine, not a managed cloud
+  platform -- ~99% uptime accepted as sufficient specifically because the
+  AH degrades gracefully (panel shows "needs a connection" and disables
+  itself; the rest of the game is entirely unaffected, same as any other
+  offline moment -- a genuinely new "this one feature needs the
+  internet" category in an otherwise fully offline-capable game).
+  Existing Cloudflare-managed domain covers TLS; an already-active
+  Dynamic DNS hostname just needs an AH subdomain pointed at it; a
+  Windows Service auto-starts the backend on boot. Postgres specifically
+  for row-locked buyout transactions -- two players clicking buyout on
+  the same listing within the same second needs an atomic check/debit/
+  transfer/mark-sold, not a race. Rough data model: `listings`
+  (sellerSteamId nullable/`SYSTEM` for system-seeded stock, full
+  rolled-stat JSON per item rather than just an id -- equipment carries
+  randomized stats/crafted mods, so a listing has to capture the actual
+  roll), `mailbox` (ownerSteamId, gold/item payload, claimed-at),
+  `transactions` (audit trail for support/dispute lookups). **Steam
+  auth:** the client already obtains a session ticket via the integrated
+  Steamworks SDK (`GetAuthSessionTicket`) -- proven working by the live
+  Steam leaderboards -- but backend verification of that ticket via
+  Steam's `AuthenticateUserTicket` Web API is **not yet built**;
+  leaderboards only prove the client-side flow, not that a game-owned
+  backend can validate a ticket (leaderboard uploads go straight to
+  Valve's servers today, bypassing any backend of ours). DevTools
+  (already its own standalone local app, separate from the shipped
+  client, so no exposure risk) gets a new "AH Admin" tab: connection/DDNS
+  health, manual force-rotation of system-seeded stock, transaction
+  search/audit lookup, manual trade reversal, per-Steam-ID suspension --
+  talking to the backend's own `/admin/*` routes behind an admin token
+  that lives only in local DevTools config, never the shipped client.
+
+  **Client integration:** new AH panel (browse/filter by item type, slot,
+  rarity, level; active-listings management; mailbox UI). List flow pulls
+  the item from local inventory only *after* the server confirms the
+  listing was created -- no optimistic strip on the assumption the
+  network call succeeds. Buy flow: item to buyer's inventory immediately,
+  seller's gold to their mailbox. System-seeded listings render
+  identically to real ones (same underlying data shape).
+
+  **Genuine blockers (infra, not design):** Steam session-ticket
+  verification on the backend; the row-locked buyout transaction
+  (approach scoped, not built); the Postgres backup routine (hardware --
+  a backup drive plus a separate external data drive -- already exists on
+  the server, the automated dump/rotation script doesn't).
+
+  **Still open, needs another pass before implementation:** exact
+  deposit-fee/sale-cut percentages; system-seeded stock framing
+  (permanent padding vs. tapering as real volume grows); whether the 48h
+  tier is its own Guild upgrade or folded into the access-gating one;
+  exact listing cap; whether "Black Market" as a label should be renamed
+  now that it's a real player marketplace lane (e.g. "Broker's Stock") --
+  purely thematic, not urgent.
+
+  **Build order once started:** host prep (subdomain -> DDNS, Postgres
+  installed, empty Windows Service scaffolding) -> backend skeleton (an
+  empty `/health` route reachable over HTTPS, proving the whole DNS/TLS/
+  service/firewall chain before any real logic) -> Steam ticket
+  verification (the one genuine remaining blocker -- nothing downstream
+  can be built against a "real player" concept without it) -> core
+  listings + buyout (the row-locked transaction, proven via the bare
+  API/DevTools before any client UI exists) -> system-seeded stock
+  (reuses the core listings code path) -> mailbox system -> client AH
+  panel (built last, depends on everything above being stable) ->
+  DevTools AH Admin tab (can run in parallel with the panel once the
+  backend/listings exist) -> backup routine + hardening, last, once
+  there's real data worth protecting. Suggested starting point: host prep
+  and the backend skeleton together -- get an empty backend live and
+  reachable at the domain before any real game logic sits behind it.
+
+  **Flagged, not resolved:** `guild-idler-project-brief.md` still says
+  "Guild Idler" throughout -- this doc and `package.json`'s
+  `productName` already use **Guildbound**; the brief needs its own pass
+  to catch up. Base price also needs reconciling there -- this entry and
+  the Post-launch DLC strategy entry below now reflect **$9** as current,
+  up from the $6.99 previously on record; confirm against the brief and
+  update it to match.
+
